    {
      "dom_id": "question-38680",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14253392/assets/682105815047990048e100ea59c2652e48c0deea8c8caa422bfe3efca7d11c65.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are correct?\n\nI. $p(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nII. $x=-1$ is a simple root.\n\nIII. $x=2$ is a root of even multiplicity.",
      "worked_solution": "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. As $x$ moves right in the positive direction towards $+\\infty ,$ the $y$ value moves down in the negative direction towards $-\\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is false. The root $x=-1$ is not a simple root because the graph crosses the $x$-axis at the point, and the curve is tangent to the $x$-axis at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. The root $x=2$ has an odd multiplicity because the graph passes through the $x$-axis at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\".",
